sources read:
  samples/fixtures/loop_flags.clean.jsonl

attribution coverage: 5 of 5 assistant turns were usage-bearing; 0 carried no recognised usage field
usage-field presence across usage-bearing turns (a field missing here is a silent 0, not a measured one):
  input_tokens: 5/5
  output_tokens: 5/5
  cache_creation_input_tokens: 5/5
  cache_read_input_tokens: 5/5
sidechain (subagent) turns: 0

TOTAL over 5 usage-bearing turns: in=5 out=50 cache_create=500 cache_read=5000
cache_read : output overhead = 100.00

BY TOOL (turn-grain: a turn's usage is attributed to the SET of tools it called, not split per call):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  tools
      4       40        4000          400  Bash
      1       10        1000          100  (no tool call)

BY AGENT (same-session Task subagents separated from the main thread):
  turns      out  cache_read cache_create  agent
      5       50        5000          500  main thread

TOP 5 TURNS BY cache_read (the turns re-reading the most cached context):
   cache_read      out  agent / tools
         1000       10  main thread / Bash
         1000       10  main thread / Bash
         1000       10  main thread / Bash
         1000       10  main thread / (no tool call)
         1000       10  main thread / Bash

LOOP FLAGS
  Mechanical signals, computed from transcript STRUCTURE only -- turn boundaries and
  tool_use block counts -- never from judging what was said. THESE ARE FLAGS, NOT
  VERDICTS: a flagged span means an unusual amount of work by the threshold YOU set,
  which is a pointer to look. It is not a finding that the work was wasted, and no
  threshold here is a validated constant.

  real user turns (span boundaries): 2
  user entries NOT counted as operator input, by reason:
    isMeta: 1
      isMeta:true -- a prompt the engine synthesised, e.g. the relaunch stub "Continue
      from where you left off."
    tool_result: 4
      carries tool_result blocks only -- the engine handing a tool's output back, which
      is the overwhelming majority of user entries in a real transcript

  SIGNAL 1 -- tool calls per span between consecutive real user turns
    spans: 2  tool calls per span: p50=1 p90=3 max=3  (assistant turns per span: p50=1 max=4)
    threshold: > 5 tool calls -- caller-set, strictly greater, so a span of exactly 5 is NOT flagged
    FLAGGED spans: 0 of 2 -- a MEASURED clean result against this threshold

  SIGNAL 2 -- repeated / near-duplicate invocations: NOT IMPLEMENTED
      NOT IMPLEMENTED -- repeated/near-duplicate invocations. Published work rates this
      the most frequent of 14 multi-agent failure modes (MAST FM-1.3, step repetition,
      15.7% over 1600+ annotated traces), so it is the next signal to add, not one that
      was judged unimportant. It is deferred rather than approximated: matching on tool
      NAME alone would report a Bash-heavy session as one long repetition, and the
      command-level clustering that makes it meaningful is being built separately.
      Nothing partial is reported under its name.

  SIGNAL 3 -- consecutive assistant turns that called no tool
    longest streak anywhere: 1 turn(s)
    main thread: 5 turns, 1 with no tool call, in 1 streak(s), longest=1
    sidechains: 0 turns, 0 with no tool call, in 0 streak(s), longest=0
    threshold: > 3 consecutive turns -- caller-set, strictly greater
    FLAGGED streaks: 0 -- a MEASURED clean result against this threshold
